# RUN: llc -mtriple=haydn-unknown-elf -global-isel-abort=1 \
# RUN:   -run-pass=finalize-isel -verify-machineinstrs %s -o - \
# RUN:   | FileCheck %s

# Role: MIR — B3 — MULSSH/MULSUH/MULUUH are NON-destructive.

# REGRESSION TEST: B3 — MULSSH/MULSUH/MULUUH are NON-destructive.
#
# Bug: Legacy MULSSH/MULSUH/MULUUH defs (HaydnInstrInfoAuto.td) had a spurious
# `let Constraints = "$rd = $rs2"` (destructive tied-def). Golden
# (slot1_mac_instruction_list.json) says these are NON-destructive: rt, rs1, rs2
# are distinct (GPR_Write_Port=[rt], GPR_Read_Port=[rs1,rs2], no tie). The
# spurious tie forced a TwoAddress MOV copy and corrupted the result when the
# caller passed the same register for rt and rs2 with different intent.
#
# Fix (B3): REMOVE the `let Constraints = "$rd = $rs2"` from the 3 legacy
# defs. `(outs GPR32:$rt), (ins GPR32:$rs1, GPR32:$rs2)` — no tie.
#
# Test design: MIR with a MULSSH where rt, rs1, rs2 are distinct vregs. Before
# the fix, the tied constraint forced rt==rs2 (a copy), which a strict verifier
# flags as a constraint violation when they're distinct. After the fix, no tie.

---
name:            test_mulssh_nondestructive
tracksRegLiveness: true
selected: true
body:             |
  bb.0.entry:
    %0:gpr32 = IMPLICIT_DEF
    %1:gpr32 = IMPLICIT_DEF
    %2:gpr32 = MULSSH %0:gpr32, %1:gpr32
    $r0 = COPY %2:gpr32
    RET implicit $r0, implicit $r15
...

# CHECK-LABEL: name: test_mulssh_nondestructive
# CHECK: MULSSH %0, %1
# CHECK-NOT: tied
